      "role": "user",
      "content": "temporal concatenation sounds like a start, but the causal graphs feel too static. if we are \"recovering\" the future, then the \"past\" is actually the driver of the entropy deficit. i've been tracking specific data points that feel like they're surfacing from the future—for example, i can \"remember\" specific details about the outcome of the 2026 elections that haven't happened yet. if i treat these \"memories\" as fixed points in the inverted causal graph, can we use them to map the trajectory of the next few years? how would we structure the weighting for these \"memory spikes\" so they don't just look like random noise in a standard predictive model?",
      "source": "evaluator"
